{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>I am YHWH your Elohim.</b> This utterance is a positive commandment. He said <b>I am YHWH</b>, instructing and commanding them that they should know and believe that there is YHWH, and that He is Elohim to them — that is to say, existent, preexistent, from Him was everything in will and in power, and He is Elohim to them, meaning they are obligated to serve Him. And He said <b>who brought you out of the land of Egypt</b>, because their departure from there teaches about [His] existence and about [His] will, for with knowledge and providence from Him we went out from there; and it also teaches about innovation [creation ex nihilo], for if the world were eternal, nothing would change from its nature; and it teaches about [His] power, and power teaches about [His] unity, as He said (Exodus 9:14): \"in order that you may know that there is none like Me in all the earth.\" And this is the reason for \"who brought you out\" — for they are the ones who know and are witnesses to all these things." "And the reason for <b>from the house of slaves</b> is that they stood in Egypt in a house of slaves, captive to Pharaoh, and He said this to them to indicate that they are obligated that this great, exalted, and awesome Name should be for them as Elohim, whom they must serve, for He redeemed them from the slavery of Egypt, as in the meaning of \"they are My servants whom I brought out of the land of Egypt\" (Leviticus 25:55). And I have already hinted above (Exodus 19:20) at the reason for the two holy Names by way of truth. And this commandment is called in the words of our Sages (Berakhot 33a) acceptance of the yoke of the kingdom of Heaven, for these expressions that I have mentioned pertain to a king in relation to the people. And thus they said in the Mekhilta (Exodus 20:3): \"<b>There shall be no other elohim before My face</b>\" — why is this said? Because it says \"<b>I am YHWH your Elohim</b>\" — a parable of a king who entered a province. His servants said to him: Decree decrees upon us. He said to them: No. When you accept my kingship I will decree decrees upon you, for if you do not accept my kingship, how do you fulfill my decrees? Thus the Place said to Israel: \"<b>I am YHWH your Elohim</b>\" — \"<b>There shall not be</b>\" — I am He whose kingship you accepted upon yourselves in Egypt. They said to Him: Yes. Since you accepted My kingship, accept My decrees — that is to say, after you accept upon yourselves and acknowledge that I am YHWH and I am your Elohim from the land of Egypt, accept all My commandments.\nAnd all the utterances, all of them, were stated in singular language: \"YHWH your Elohim who brought you out,\" and not as He began to speak to them: <b>You have seen</b>, etc., <b>if you will indeed obey</b> (Exodus 19:4–5) — to warn that each and every individual among them would be punished for the commandments, for with each one He would speak, and to each one He would command, so that they would not think that He follows the majority and the individual would escape along with them. And Moses explained this intention to them at the end of the Torah in the portion <b>You are standing</b> (Deuteronomy 29:17)."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:17:24.191172862Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}